{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas-requirements` skill MUST be the home of drafting, reviewing, and agreeing requirements, the requirement review criteria, the procedure for choosing references, and the criteria the assessment applies to code and tests.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The reviewed test adequately checks the subject.\n\n**Obligations.** `requirements.cli.skill_structure.requirements` requires `habeas-requirements` to be the home of six topics:\n- drafting requirements;\n- reviewing requirements;\n- agreeing requirements;\n- the requirement review criteria;\n- the procedure for choosing references;\n- the criteria the assessment applies to code and tests.\n\nThe cited parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure` adds that each topic is stated in full in exactly one skill, so no other skill may state it.\n\n**Test and assertions.** The single `{test}` item is `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_is_home_of_drafting_review_references_and_criteria]`.\n- **Drafting, reviewing and agreeing.** The test loops over `workflow_steps(\"habeas-requirements\")`. For each topic it asserts that some numbered Workflow step contains the phrase \"Draft. Write drafts\", \"Review. Sort the new and changed requirements\", or \"**Gate:**\". `workflow_steps` parses only the numbered steps of the `## Workflow` section, so the text has to be in that section. Each topic is checked separately.\n- **Choosing references.** `assert_text` checks that the `## Choosing references` section, extracted by `section`, contains \"A *root* is\". `section` panics if the heading is missing, and `assert_text` is a real `assert!`.\n- **Criteria for code and tests.** The `## What the review checks` section must contain \"obligations, boundaries, and failure cases no test runs and asserts\" and the supervising-agent sentence about checking that the code fulfills each requirement. Together these cover the test-adequacy criteria and the code-fulfillment check.\n- **Requirement review criteria.** The `## Criteria` section of `references/review-prompt.md`, in the same skill, must contain **Compound:**, **Checkable:** and **One reading:**.\n- **Exactly one home.** `assert_home(\"habeas-requirements\")` does two things.\n  - It asserts that the skill's own bundle (`SKILL.md` plus `references/`, via `bundled_skill`) contains every marker from `home_markers`. These include the draft, subagent-review and gate phrases, the root definition, \"## What the review checks\", \"# Requirement group review\" and the Compound criterion.\n  - It asserts that none of the other four skills contains any of those markers.\n  - The markers cover all six topics, so a restatement elsewhere would fail the test.\n\n**Assessment.** The expected results match the requirement text, and the assertions are real. The exclusivity check relies on verbatim marker phrases, so it would not catch a paraphrased restatement. That is a reasonable way to test \"stated in full in one skill\" and is not a gap the text names. I found no untested obligation, no contradictory assertion and no marking gap, and no `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "f159a23beb16749e13e1d1de7c5be2c78dd6bcfcbb8168129bbd0ec2bacc21bf",
